decisions:
  - id: TD-2026-05-28-media-bin
    decision: Drop-anywhere ingest no longer auto-creates docs. Files land in media/, compose is an explicit verb.
    rationale: FCP/Premiere mental model fits creator workflow. The current 'drop file = new doc' conflated acquiring with composing. Dispatcher matrix collapses; UX becomes a clear two-step (acquire then compose).
  - id: TD-2026-05-28-transcribe-on-bin-add
    decision: Auto-transcribe runs on bin-add (background), not on compose
    rationale: Hides whisper latency. By the time the creator drags a clip onto canvas, transcript is usually ready. Local CPU only — no API cost. Transcripts are media-bin metadata, not doc metadata.
  - id: TD-2026-05-28-timeline-read-only-v1
    decision: v1.0 bottom timeline ships as read-only (click to select, click ruler to seek). Drag-to-reposition + trim defer to v1.1
    rationale: Smallest viable scope that's still genuinely useful (creator can SEE the timeline). Avoids mouse-state-machine complexity. Shipping editing affordances was the right call to defer.
  - id: TD-2026-05-28-sidebar-drag-required
    decision: Sidebar MEDIA → canvas/timeline drag promoted from v1.1 nice-to-have to v1.0 required
    rationale: Without it, composing with already-binned media was literally impossible. The bin model creates this requirement.
learnings:
  - The 'acquire vs compose' distinction is a real architectural pattern — separating those two verbs unlocks downstream simplifications (dispatcher matrix collapse, transcribe latency hiding, empty-project legitimacy).
  - 'Aegis''s ''phase: done envelope is missing the fields the browser checks for'' catch was a real blocker that would have shipped a broken toast feature. Cross-builder review (Judge) caught it before commit. The roster pattern (parallel build + parallel review) works.'
  - Stale Node processes hold OLD code in memory — server-side fixes need a restart, not just a browser hard-refresh. Worth a tooling pass to auto-restart on dist changes for next session.
  - Autonomous loop execution (kick off S0 → wave → lore → commit → push → next wave) sustains forward motion without re-asking. The team-routing memory + clear task tracking made this feel like one coherent session despite spanning 5+ separate agent runs.
